    definition: 'An open protocol that standardizes how AI applications connect to external tools and data — often described as a USB-C port for AI context.',
    simpleExplanation: 'One plug that lets any AI app talk to any tool or data source, instead of every app building its own integrations.',
    detailedExplanation: 'MCP follows a client–server model. An MCP host (an app like Claude Desktop or an IDE) connects to MCP servers, each of which exposes three primitives: tools the model can call, resources it can read, and prompts it can use. Because the protocol is standard, a server built once — for GitHub, Slack, a database — works inside every MCP-compatible client, turning an N×M integration problem into N+M. Created by Anthropic in late 2024, it has since spread across major AI vendors as the de facto standard for model–tool connectivity.',
    analogy: 'USB-C for AI: before it, every device needed its own cable; after it, one connector powers everything.',
